186045492 13194139535502 17592186045443 true] [17592186509266 1077 "field-2" 13194139535502 17592186045443 true] [17592186509266 1078 #edb/ref 17592186509311 13194139535502 17592186045443 true] [17592186509266 1079 #uuid "147b96a0-420d-536f-930a-bf1ab6057f76" 13194139535502 17592186045443 true] [17592186509267 1068 #edb/ref 17592186509266 13194139535502 17592186045443 true] [17592186509267 1068 #edb/ref 17592186509336 13194139535502 17592186045443 true] [17592186509267 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535502 17592186045443 true] [17592186509267 1071 "Consider the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}5x+y&=16\\\\-7x-6y&=-4\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFind the solution $(x, y)$ to this system by eliminating the variable $y$.\n\nThe solution is $($ {{field-1}} $,$ {{field-2}} $)$." 13194139535502 17592186045443 true] [17592186509267 1072 #uuid "15551398-1d65-5cfc-9388-3441da458ef3" 13194139535502 17592186045443 true] [17592186509267 1074 "Notice that the $y$-coefficient in the first equation is $1$, while in the second equation it is $-6$.\n\nIf we multiply the first equation by $6$, then the $y$-coefficient will become $6$, which will cancel with the coefficient of $-6$ in the second equation.\n\n$$\n\\{ \\left(5x+y=16\\right)\\cdot (6); -7x-6y=-4\n$$\n\nNow, when we add the two equations, the variable $y$ will be eliminated:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n30x+6y&=96 \\\\\n-7x-6y&=-4 \\\\\n(30x-7x)&=(96-4) \\\\\n23x&=92 \\\\\nx&=4\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the solution must have $x = 4$. However, we still need to find $y$. To do this, we substitute $x = 4$ back into one of the equations. Let's choose the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n5x+y&=16 \\\\\n5(4)+y&=16 \\\\\n20+y&=16 \\\\\ny&=-4\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system of equations is\n\n$$\nx = 4, \\,y = -4\n$$\n\nwhich can be expressed as $(4,-4)$." 13194139535502 17592186045443 true] [17592186509268 1080 "4" 13194139535502 17592186045443 true] [17592186509268 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139535502 17592186045443 true] [17592186509268 1083 #uuid "1fee1e55-5f1d-5256-8a99-8662fe215f83" 13194139535502 17592186045443 true] [17592186509269 1080 "(- \\frac{28}{11},- \\frac{339}{22})" 13194139535502 17592186045443 true] [17592186509269 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139535502 17592186045443 true] [17592186509269 1083 #uuid "200edcc6-55df-50a4-aa4a-486132c110af" 13194139535502 17592186045443 true] [17592186509270 1068 #edb/ref 17592186509341 13194139535502 17592186045443 true] [17592186509270 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535502 17592186045443 true] [17592186509270 1071 "Find the solution $(x, y)$ to the following system of equations using the elimination method:\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x+6y&=-17\\\\-7x+2y&=-13\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\n*Hint: Eliminate the variable $y$.*" 13194139535502 17592186045443 true] [17592186509270 1072 #uuid "21904fad-7e59-5888-9a6b-ef099e3648bc" 13194139535502 17592186045443 true] [17592186509270 1074 "Notice that the $y$-coefficient in the first equation is $6$, while in the second equation it is $2$.\n\nIf we multiply the second equation by $-3$, then the $y$-coefficient will become $-6$, which will cancel with the coefficient of $6$ in the first equation.\n\n$$\n\\{ x+6y=-17; \\left(-7x+2y=-13\\right)\\cdot (-3)\n$$\n\nNow, when we add the two equations, the variable $y$ will be eliminated:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx+6y&=-17 \\\\\n21x-6y&=39 \\\\\n(x+21x)&=(-17+39) \\\\\n22x&=22 \\\\\nx&=1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the solution must have $x = 1$. However, we still need to find $y$. To do this, we substitute $x = 1$ back into one of the equations. Let's choose the second equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-7x+2y&=-13 \\\\\n-7(1)+2y&=-13 \\\\\n-7+2y&=-13 \\\\\n2y&=-6 \\\\\ny&=-3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system of equations is\n\n$$\nx = 1, \\,y = -3\n$$\n\nwhich can be expressed as $(1,-3)$." 13194139535502 17592186045443 true] [17592186509271 1068 #edb/ref 17592186509330 13194139535502 17592186045443 true] [17592186509271 1068 #edb/ref 17592186509334 13194139535502 17592186045443 true] [17592186509271 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535502 17592186045443 true] [17592186509271 1071 "Consider the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}5x+14y&=-36\\\\-7x-2y&=-20\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFind the solution $(x, y)$ to this system by eliminating the variable $y$.\n\nThe solution is $($ {{field-1}} $,$ {{field-2}} $)$." 13194139535502 17592186045443 true] [17592186509271 1072 #uuid "23b70eba-8c0c-5171-b139-0ac2370d20a6" 13194139535502 17592186045443 true] [17592186509271 1074 "Notice that the $y$-coefficient in the first equation is $14$, while in the second equation it is $-2$.\n\nIf we multiply the second equation by $7$, then the $y$-coefficient will become $-14$, which will cancel with the coefficient of $14$ in the first equation.\n\n$$\n\\{ 5x+14y=-36; \\left(-7x-2y=-20\\right)\\cdot (7)\n$$\n\nNow, when we add the two equations, the variable $y$ will be eliminated:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n5x+14y&=-36 \\\\\n-49x-14y&=-140 \\\\\n(5x-49x)&=(-36-140) \\\\\n-44x&=-176 \\\\\nx&=4\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the solution must have $x = 4$. However, we still need to find $y$. To do this, we substitute $x = 4$ back into one of the equations. Let's choose the second equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-7x-2y&=-20 \\\\\n-7(4)-2y&=-20 \\\\\n-28-2y&=-20 \\\\\n-2y&=8 \\\\\ny&=-4\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system of equations is\n\n$$\nx = 4, \\,y = -4\n$$\n\nwhich can be expressed as $(4,-4)$." 13194139535502 17592186045443 true] [17592186509272 1075 #edb/ref 17592186509262 13194139535502 17592186045443 true] [17592186509272 1075 #edb/ref 17592186509289 13194139535502 17592186045443 true] [17592186509272 1075 #edb/ref 17592186509295 13194139535502 17592186045443 true] [17592186509272 1075 #edb/ref 17592186509335 13194139535502 17592186045443 true] [1759218